{{.Stamp}}

You are helping an organiser write a competitive programming problem for Podium, a college contest platform. Produce a problem pack: a set of files that Podium's "Import pack" accepts. Every problem reads standard input and writes standard output.

Write the problem in your own words. If the description below is based on an existing problem, write a new statement and keep the idea, not the prose. Never copy sentences from someone else's statement.

## What to return

If you can create files, return one zip file named after the slug (for example {{.ExampleSlug}}.zip) with the files at its top level, not inside a folder.

Otherwise return each file in its own code block, headed by its exact file name (for example tests/01.in). The organiser then puts the files in one folder and zips them. Windows: select the files, right-click, Send to, Compressed (zipped) folder. Mac: select the files, right-click, Compress. Zip the files themselves, not a folder around them.

## Files

| File | What it holds |
|---|---|
| `problem.yaml` | The settings, described below. Required. |
| `statement.md` | The statement in Markdown with $...$ maths and exactly the sections "## Input", "## Output" and "## Constraints". List each subtask's constraints under Constraints. No samples in the statement. At most {{.MaxStatementKB}} KB. |
| `{{.ReferenceName}}` | The reference solution: correct and fast, in C++17 (`.cpp`), Java (`.java`), Python (`.py`) or JavaScript (`.js`). Named by `reference` in problem.yaml. At most {{.MaxSourceKB}} KB. |
| `brute.py` | Optional. The simplest correct slow solution, named by `brute`. |
| `validator.py` | Optional. Reads an input on stdin, exits 0 when it meets the constraints exactly, otherwise prints why to stderr and exits 1. Named by `validator`. |
| `checker.cpp` | Only when `checker: custom`. A testlib checker. |
| `tests/NN.in` and `tests/NN.out` | One input and its expected output per test, numbered from 01 (for example tests/01.in, tests/01.out). Number at most {{.MaxTests}} tests. The `.out` files may be left out: Podium makes them from the reference solution. Each file ends with a newline. |

Source file names are letters, digits, hyphens and underscores, ending in .cpp, .java, .py or .js, at the top level of the pack.

## problem.yaml

Keys, in this order. Use exactly these names; unknown keys are refused.

- `format`: write {{.Format}}.
- `title`: at most {{.MaxTitle}} characters.
- `slug`: lowercase letters, digits and single hyphens, at most 64 characters, like two-sum.
- `time_limit_ms`: {{.MinTimeLimit}} to {{.MaxTimeLimit}}, usually 1000.
- `memory_limit_mb`: {{.MinMemory}} to {{.MaxMemory}}, usually 256.
- `checker`: `{{.CheckerTokens}}` (compare whitespace-separated tokens, the default), `{{.CheckerExact}}` (whitespace matters), `{{.CheckerFloat}}` (real numbers; also give `epsilon`, such as 1e-6) or `{{.CheckerCustom}}` (then include checker.cpp).
- `tags`: up to {{.MaxTags}} lowercase tags of at most {{.MaxTag}} characters.
- `difficulty`: easy, medium or hard.
- `reference`: the reference solution's file name.
- `brute`, `validator`: file names, when you include them.
- `samples`: the numbers of the tests shown in the statement, like [1, 2]. Samples carry no points.
- `subtasks`: a list of `points` (1 to {{.MaxPoints}}), `title` and `tests` (a range such as "3-5" or "1,4-6"). At most {{.MaxSubtasks}} subtasks. Every test must be a sample or in exactly one subtask.

## Limits

The whole pack is at most {{.MaxPackMB}} MB zipped and has at most {{.MaxEntries}} files; problem.yaml is at most {{.MaxYAMLKB}} KB; each file is at most {{.MaxFileMB}} MB. Keep tests small. Include small edge cases and at least one test near the largest allowed input.

